<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1113">1113</a>–1151</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Geoffrey V of Anjou, Geoffrey Plantagenet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and Maine, married Matilda, daughter of Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in 1128. He conquered Normandy in the 1140s while Matilda fought Stephen for the English throne. Their son Henry II joined Anjou, Normandy, England and Aquitaine into the Angevin Empire, and the Plantagenet name derives from Geoffrey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-plantagenet-count-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou" width="320" height="611" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original creater of enamel unknown. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geoffrey_of_Anjou_Monument.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Geoffrey was born on 24 August 1113, the elder son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk V of Anjou</a> and Ermengarde of Maine. The chronicler John of Marmoutier described him as handsome, red haired, jovial and a great warrior. King Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, hearing of his prowess, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> to Anjou to arrange a marriage with his 25-year-old daughter Matilda, and on 10 June 1128 he knighted the 15-year-old Geoffrey in preparation for the wedding.</p><p>The marriage was meant to seal a lasting peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Normandy and Anjou. Matilda was eleven years older than Geoffrey and proud of her status as dowager empress. Their relationship was stormy but happy, with long separations, and she returned to her father's court after a few months until Henry I intervened. They had three sons: Henry, Geoffrey and William.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Geoffrey's father left on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1129, leaving him as count of Anjou. When Henry I died in 1135 Geoffrey supported Matilda's entry into Normandy to claim her inheritance, but her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">Stephen of Blois</a> had been crowned king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and many barons in Normandy followed him. Matilda landed in England in 1139 with 140 knights, and in the Anarchy that followed Stephen was captured at Lincoln in February 1141.</p><p>Geoffrey meanwhile secured Normandy west and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> during 1142 and 1143, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> on 14 January 1144 and took the title of Duke of Normandy that summer. He held the duchy until 1149, when he and Matilda ceded it to their son Henry, which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> ratified the next year. He put down baronial rebellions in Anjou in 1129, 1135 and from 1145 to 1151, and imprisoned his younger brother Elias, who died in 1151. The risk of revolt slowed his progress in Normandy and is one reason he could never assist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Geoffrey died suddenly on 7 September 1151, aged 38, after a fever struck him as he returned from a royal council. He made bequests of gifts and charities at Château-du-Loir and was buried in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>. His son Henry succeeded him. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallingford">Wallingford</a> in 1153 Stephen was to remain king for life with Henry as his successor, beginning the Plantagenet era in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The name Plantagenet was taken for the dynasty long after his death from his epithet, perhaps from the sprig of broom, the genêt, that he wore in his hat.</p><p>An enamel funerary plaque commissioned by his widow shows him with a blue shield bearing gold lions, one of the earliest examples of European heraldry. The chronicler Jean de Marmentier reported that Henry I granted him a badge of gold lions at his knighting in 1128.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geoffrey V of Anjou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Matilda’s husband <b>Geoffrey Plantagenet</b>, count of Anjou, conquered the duchy, entering Rouen in 1144, and in 1150 he handed it to their son Henry, who became King Henry II of England in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry II inherited Anjou, Maine and Touraine from his father <b>Geoffrey Plantagenet</b> and Normandy through his mother Matilda, gained Aquitaine by marrying Eleanor of Aquitaine in 1152, and became king of England in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/state/angevin-empire">Angevin Empire</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Geoffrey Plantagenet</b> conquered Normandy in the 1140s, and his son Henry II joined Anjou to England and Aquitaine in the Angevin Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-anjou">County of Anjou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Château-du-Loir, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.55&amp;year=1137" aria-label="Angers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4968 6063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4968 6063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,-0.55&amp;year=1137">What was here in 1137? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
